<h1>Playground</h1>

<p>Write a deck on the left; the terminal presents it live — the same
deckhand core, compiled to WebAssembly. Your draft is kept in this
browser. <button id="pg-reset" class="linklike">reset to the example</button></p>

<div class="playground">
  <div>
    <div class="tui-block" data-title="deckhand · your deck">
      <div class="terminal-frame playground-frame" id="pg-terminal"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="terminal-caption" id="pg-caption"></div>
  </div>
  <textarea id="pg-editor" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off"
            aria-label="deck markdown editor"></textarea>
</div>
<p id="pg-status" class="pg-status"></p>

<details class="gist-tools">
  <summary>save / load via gist</summary>
  <form id="pg-gist-load-form" class="play-form">
    <input type="url" id="pg-gist-url" placeholder="https://gist.github.com/you/abc123"
           autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false">
    <button type="submit">load</button>
  </form>
  <form id="pg-gist-save-form" class="play-form">
    <input type="password" id="pg-gh-token" placeholder="GitHub token (gist scope only)"
           autocomplete="off">
    <label class="remember"><input type="checkbox" id="pg-token-remember"> remember</label>
    <button type="submit">save as new gist</button>
    <button type="button" id="pg-copy" class="secondary-btn">copy markdown</button>
  </form>
  <p class="fineprint">Saving creates a <em>secret</em> gist through the GitHub API with your
  token — it needs only the <code>gist</code> scope
  (<a href="https://github.com/settings/tokens" target="_blank" rel="noopener">create one here</a>)
  and never leaves your browser except to call GitHub. “Remember” stores it in this browser's
  localStorage. No token? <em>copy markdown</em> and paste at gist.github.com/new. Loading works
  token-free for public gists, including multi-file manifest gists (flattened into the editor).</p>
</details>

<p class="hint-bar">click the terminal, then <kbd>space</kbd> next ·
<kbd>o</kbd> overview · <kbd>↓</kbd>/<kbd>↑</kbd> depth — the editor keeps
your slide position while you type</p>
